KG42.register("rules-4f431fbe9124cbbe6a5febd5",{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R002","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R002","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local market clearing","process_stage":"clearing","market_meaning":"Iterative price negotiation coordinates agents' agreement on the local allocation of flexibility contracts. Scope: Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. Condition: Negotiation terminates when no price changes occur during an iteration. Deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price negotiation can produce a stable local contract allocation when agent preferences are fully substitutable. Scope: Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability. Each agent selects a subset of its available contracts that maximizes its own utility. Scope: Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. A feasible outcome associates each selected trade with at most one contract. Scope: Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. The market should produce a stable allocation: no agent prefers to drop an associated contract, and no group prefers to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts. Scope: Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. The local allocation should be Pareto efficient: no participant can be made better off without making another worse off. Scope: Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. Prosumer contract selection must respect resource power limits and energy-storage limits. Scope: Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. At the end of price negotiation, the allocation satisfies the DSO's minimum and maximum total distribution-network demand constraints for each time interval. Scope: Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. Condition: Prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints. Each aggregator's total upstream and downstream contracts must balance separately in every time interval. Scope: Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. Condition: Aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. In this extension, prosumer power and energy constraints replace the aggregator's per-interval upstream-downstream contract-balance constraint. Scope: Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. Condition: Aggregator preferences are extended to represent their own flexible energy resources. Agents first select the minimum number of contracts needed to meet their constraints, then iteratively choose available adjustments providing the greatest utility increase. Selection terminates when none of the assessed adjustments provides further utility. Scope: Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy the single improvement property.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function clearing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["clearing.local","coordination.price_signal"],"E":[{"edge_id":"v141-c-R002-E001","source":"coordination.price_signal","target":"clearing.local","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R002-C001","v141-c-R002-C002"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g02-e19"]}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"clearing","context_text":"Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. | Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. | Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers. | Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. | Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes. | Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome. | Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market. | Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. | Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. | Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model. | Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e19","v141-b-g02-e25","v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g02-e28"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"x-Xprime","symbol":"X'","meaning":"Feasible/selected set of contracts constituting the local market outcome.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"X' \\subseteq X, where X is the set of contracts between the agents; feasibility requires each trade in \\tau(X') to be associated with at most one contract.","unit":null,"indices":["trading_network"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13","v141-b-g02-e14"]},{"variable_id":"x-Xprime-i","symbol":"X'_i","meaning":"Agent i's selected subset of its available contracts.","kind":"operational","domain":"X'_i \\subseteq X_i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g02-e14"]},{"variable_id":"x-Yit","symbol":"Y_{it}","meaning":"Agent i's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{it} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_i \\mid b(\\varphi)=i, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g02-e03"]},{"variable_id":"x-Zit","symbol":"Z_{it}","meaning":"Agent i's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{it} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_i \\mid s(\\omega)=i, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g02-e03"]},{"variable_id":"x-Ydt","symbol":"Y_{dt}","meaning":"DSO d's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{dt} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_d \\mid b(\\varphi)=d, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["d","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"variable_id":"x-Zdt","symbol":"Z_{dt}","meaning":"DSO d's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{dt} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_d \\mid s(\\omega)=d, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["d","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"variable_id":"x-Yjt","symbol":"Y_{jt}","meaning":"Aggregator j's upstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Y_{jt} = \\{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_j \\mid b(\\varphi)=j, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"]},{"variable_id":"x-Zjt","symbol":"Z_{jt}","meaning":"Aggregator j's downstream contracts for time interval t.","kind":"operational","domain":"Z_{jt} = \\{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega}) \\in X_j \\mid s(\\omega)=j, t_{\\omega}=t\\}","unit":null,"indices":["j","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"theta-Xi","symbol":"X_i","meaning":"Agent i's available contracts, from which the choice correspondence selects a subset.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Agent-specific set of available contracts X_i"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E14","v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-Ui","symbol":"U_i","meaning":"Agent i's utility function over sets of contracts; source equations (4)-(6) define it for prosumers, the DSO and aggregators.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Agent-specific utility function defined by source equations (4)-(6) for the relevant agent type"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E27"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-it","symbol":"P^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Prosumer i's pre-market planned retail flexible demand for interval t, used as a fixed input in the flexibility market.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined pre-market planned flexible demand for prosumer i at interval t"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-Ei0","symbol":"E_{i0}","meaning":"Prosumer i's initial stored energy.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined initial stored energy for prosumer i"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-lower","symbol":"\\underline{P}^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Lower output-power limit for prosumer i's flexible resource at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined lower flexible power limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-Pf-upper","symbol":"\\bar{P}^{f}_{it}","meaning":"Upper output-power limit for prosumer i's flexible resource at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined upper flexible power limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-E-lower","symbol":"\\underline{E}_{i\\tau}","meaning":"Lower energy-storage limit for prosumer i at interval \\tau.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined lower energy storage limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","\\tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E22"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-E-upper","symbol":"\\bar{E}_{i\\tau}","meaning":"Upper energy-storage limit for prosumer i at interval \\tau.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined upper energy storage limit"},"unit":"per-contract units","indices":["i","\\tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E22"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-t","symbol":"P^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Predicted pre-flexibility-market retail demand at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined predicted pre-flexibility retail demand"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-lower","symbol":"\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Minimum total distribution-network demand limit at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined minimum distribution-network demand limit"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"parameter_id":"theta-PD-upper","symbol":"\\bar{P}^{D}_{t}","meaning":"Maximum total distribution-network demand limit at interval t.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"Source-defined maximum distribution-network demand limit"},"unit":null,"indices":["t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e10"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Iterative price negotiation coordinates agents' agreement on the local allocation of flexibility contracts. Scope: Completed day-ahead flexibility-contract allocation within one distribution network. Condition: Negotiation terminates when no price changes occur during an iteration.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E10"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price negotiation can produce a stable local contract allocation when agent preferences are fully substitutable. Scope: Stable-outcome guarantee for the distribution-network flexibility market. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy full substitutability.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.price_signal","clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each agent selects a subset of its available contracts that maximizes its own utility. Scope: Individual contract-bundle selection by every agent in the local flexibility market.","expression":"C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime-i"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Xi","theta-Ui"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"A feasible outcome associates each selected trade with at most one contract. Scope: Feasibility of the selected local flexibility-market outcome.","expression":"\\forall \\omega \\in \\tau(X'),\\; |\\{x \\in X' : x = (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\}| \\le 1","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e13"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","kind":"logical","role":"core","statement":"The market should produce a stable allocation: no agent prefers to drop an associated contract, and no group prefers to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts. Scope: Desired outcome of the local flexibility market for the DSO, aggregators and prosumers.","expression":"X' \\text{ is stable} \\iff \\left( X'_i \\in C_i(X')\\; \\forall i \\in A \\right) \\land \\left( \\nexists B \\subseteq X : B \\neq \\emptyset,\\; B \\text{ feasible},\\; B \\cap X' = \\emptyset,\\; B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X')\\; \\forall i \\in a(B) \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Xprime","x-Xprime-i"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Ui"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e14"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The local allocation should be Pareto efficient: no participant can be made better off without making another worse off. Scope: Efficiency requirement for local flexibility-market outcomes.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Prosumer contract selection must respect resource power limits and energy-storage limits. Scope: Prosumer contract-bundle selection in the proposed flexible-resource model.","expression":"\\underline{P}^{f}_{it} \\le P^{f}_{it} - |Y_{it}| + |Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^{f}_{it},\\; \\forall t \\in T;\\quad \\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0} - \\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}\\left(P^{f}_{it} - |Y_{it}| + |Z_{it}|\\right) \\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau},\\; \\forall \\tau \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Yit","x-Zit"],"parameter_refs":["theta-Pf-it","theta-Ei0","theta-Pf-lower","theta-Pf-upper","theta-E-lower","theta-E-upper"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"At the end of price negotiation, the allocation satisfies the DSO's minimum and maximum total distribution-network demand constraints for each time interval. Scope: Distribution-network demand feasibility of the final local flexibility allocation. Condition: Prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints.","expression":"\\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t} - |Y_{dt}| + |Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t},\\; \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Ydt","x-Zdt"],"parameter_refs":["theta-PD-t","theta-PD-lower","theta-PD-upper"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e25","v141-b-g01-E26"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Each aggregator's total upstream and downstream contracts must balance separately in every time interval. Scope: Aggregator contract allocations in the baseline flexibility-market model. Condition: Aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy.","expression":"|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|,\\; \\forall t \\in T","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":["x-Yjt","x-Zjt"],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e11"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C010","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"In this extension, prosumer power and energy constraints replace the aggregator's per-interval upstream-downstream contract-balance constraint. Scope: Optional extension of aggregator contract-bundle feasibility to aggregators with their own flexible energy resources. Condition: Aggregator preferences are extended to represent their own flexible energy resources.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R002-C011","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Agents first select the minimum number of contracts needed to meet their constraints, then iteratively choose available adjustments providing the greatest utility increase. Selection terminates when none of the assessed adjustments provides further utility. Scope: Individual utility-maximizing contract-selection procedure within local clearing. Condition: Agent preferences satisfy the single improvement property.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e27","v141-b-g02-e28"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Even when acting autonomously and making individually rational decisions, the DSO, aggregators and prosumers should reach agreement on a stable outcome—a set of individually beneficial transactions no group wishes to mutually deviate from."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p02-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"I. Introduction; design requirements","anchor_text":"g g q ve r The ﬂexibility market should be opt-in for prosumers, and should account for their incent","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Market outcomes should satisfy Pareto efficiency, meaning that it should not be possible to make one participant better off without making another worse off."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let \\(S \\subseteq A\\) be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator \\(j \\in S\\) incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, \\(c_{1j} \\ge 0\\). In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_j\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_j\\), aggregator \\(j\\)'s utility function is given by, \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_j}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_j}p_\\phi - c_{1j}|Y_j|,\\) if \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_j(Y_j\\cup Z_j)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise} \\tag{6}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences; IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle situations where the aggregators have their own flexible energy resources (e.g. community-scale energy storage systems [50]). In this case, the upstream-downstream contract balance constraint \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\) would be replaced by the power and energy constraints from the prosumer preferences (4)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"contracts associated with each time interval t ∈T , given by, ene ing Yit = {(φ, tφ, pφ) ∈Xi|b(φ) = ","equation_id":"(3)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Each agent i \\in A has a utility function U_i which describes its preferences over sets of contracts. The subset of contracts selected by the agent are given by its choice correspondence, C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i). \\tag{3}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, d \\in A, which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, [\\underline{P}^{D}_{t}, \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}]. The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand P^{D}_{t}, t \\in T, violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts Y_d and downstream contracts Z_d, the DSO's utility function is given by, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_d} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_d} p_{\\varphi}, \\text{if } \\underline{P}^{D}_{t} \\le P^{D}_{t}-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^{D}_{t}, \\forall t\\in T, U_d(Y_d \\cup Z_d) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"Uj(Yj ∪Zj) = −∞, otherwise (6) The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle sit- uations whe","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. Market Stability and Efficiency","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 3, MAY 2019 xi- A key aim for market design is to e","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Deﬁnition 1: An outcome X' is stable if it is: 1) Individually Rational: X'_i \\in C_i(X') for all i \\in A. 2) Unblocked: There is no feasible non-empty set B \\subseteq X, such that B \\cap X' = \\emptyset and B_i \\subseteq C_i(B \\cup X'), \\forall i \\in a(B). In other words, an outcome is stable as long as no agent would prefer to drop a contract they are associated with, and no group of agents would prefer to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"V. Price Negotiation and Contract Selection","anchor_text":"(6) V. PRICE NEGOTIATION AND CONTRACT SELECTION sit- rgy The fact that the agent preferences are ful","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The fact that the agent preferences are fully substitutable has two further consequences. First, the trading network can reach agreement on a stable outcome using the deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price-negotiation mechanism from [54]. Second, agents can use a computationally scalable process to select sets of contracts that will maximize their utility, since their preferences satisfy the single improvement property [55]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e25","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.A Price-Negotiation Mechanism","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2133 The negotiation is complete when no price changes occur during an iterati","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that if the prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints, then the constraints will be satisfied at the end of the price-negotiation. The DSO will continue to offer higher prices for unrealized contracts required to meet its demand constraints, as long as the constraints are not satisfied. Eventually, these contracts will become sufficiently valuable, so that they are also selected by the associated prosumers and aggregators."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"contract. Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the m. following process to sele","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets. A flow chart of the contract selection process for the prosumers is shown in Fig. 3. First, the agents need to ensure their constraints are not violated. The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated. The prosumers should identify the time intervals when their energy storage limits are violated, and should clear each violation in order from earliest to latest. To clear maximum energy violations, prosumers should select downstream contracts for preceding intervals, ensuring they do not introduce new power or energy constraint violations. The downstream contracts with the highest prices available at each suitable time interval should be compared, to see which gives the greatest increase in marginal utility for the unconstrained utility function. Similarly, the prosumers should select low priced upstream constraints to clear minimum energy level violations. Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e28","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p07-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"V.B Contract Selection","anchor_text":"on The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 3","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of these options provides further utility, a utility maximizing subset of contracts has been selected."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E14","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"in demand during a particular time interval. Note that all trades ere specify the same energy quanti","equation_id":"(1)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"These can be divided into a set of upstream contracts \\(Y_i\\), and a set of downstream contracts \\(Z_i\\), \\(Y_i = \\{y \\in X_i | b(y)=i\\}, \\quad Z_i = \\{z \\in X_i | s(z)=i\\}. \\tag{1}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"contracts associated with each time interval t ∈T , given by, ene ing Yit = {(φ, tφ, pφ) ∈Xi|b(φ) = ","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"These can be further divided into upstream and downstream contracts associated with each time interval \\(t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), given by, \\(Y_{it} = \\{(\\phi, t_\\phi, p_\\phi) \\in X_i | b(\\phi)=i, t_\\phi=t\\},\\) \\(Z_{it} = \\{(\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega) \\in X_i | s(\\omega)=i, t_\\omega=t\\}. \\tag{2}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"ON SYSTEM FLEXIBILITY 2131 es than energy obtained in the wholesale market [45]. Therefore, gy it is","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Prosumer \\(i\\)'s flexible energy resource is modeled as a flexible load with energy storage capacity. Let \\(E_{i0}\\) be the initial stored energy and for each time interval \\(t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), let \\([\\underline{P}^f_{it}, \\bar{P}^f_{it}]\\) be the output power limits and let \\([\\underline{E}_{it}, \\bar{E}_{it}]\\) be the energy storage limits. A range of flexible loads can be modeled using these time varying constraints, for example electric vehicles which must be recharged during certain hours."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"Additional costs terms can be used to model prosumer pref es erences associated with different types","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Before engaging with the flexibility market, the prosumer calculates the optimal schedule for its flexible energy resource in the retail market, \\(P^f_{it}, t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), considering its constraints and preferences. This is envisaged as being automated and completed by an energy management system based on user controlled settings (see e.g. [44]). Note that a key component of an energy management system is a tool for local demand forecasting [49]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"energy management system is a tool for local demand forecast- ing [49]. In the ﬂexibility market, fo","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts \\(Y_i\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_i\\), prosumer \\(i\\)'s utility function is, \\(U_i(Y_i \\cup Z_i) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_i}(p_\\omega+\\gamma_i t_\\omega) - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_i}(p_\\phi+\\gamma_i t_\\phi) + \\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}} [ p^s_{it}[P^f_{it}-P^d_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|]^+ - p^b_{it}[P^d_{it}-P^f_{it}+|Y_{it}|-|Z_{it}|]^+ - c_{2i}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)^2 ],\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^f_{it} \\le P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}| \\le \\bar{P}^f_{it}, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(\\underline{E}_{i\\tau} \\le E_{i0}-\\sum_{t=1}^{\\tau}(P^f_{it}-|Y_{it}|+|Z_{it}|)\\le \\bar{E}_{i\\tau}, \\forall \\tau\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_i(Y_i\\cup Z_i)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{4}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.A Prosumer Preferences","anchor_text":"ls Ui(Yi ∪Zi) = −∞, otherwise. (4) et s, Quantities have per-contract units. For example, if each co","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"exception"},"source_text":"Quantities have per-contract units. For example, if each contract is associated with 0.5 kWh of energy, then \\(P^f_{it}, P^d_{it}, E_{i0}\\) have units of 0.5 kWh, \\(p^s_{it}, p^b_{it}\\) have units of £/0.5 kWh and \\(\\gamma_i\\) has units of £/0.5 kWh/h."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.B DSO Preferences","anchor_text":"B. DSO Preferences The trading network has a single DSO, d ∈A, which must pr manage minimum and maxi","equation_id":"(5)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The trading network has a single DSO, \\(d \\in A\\), which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval, \\([\\underline{P}^D_t, \\bar{P}^D_t]\\). The DSO buys flexibility contracts for time intervals when the predicted pre-flexibility market retail demand \\(P^D_t, t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\), violates the maximum demand limits, and can sell contracts during unconstrained intervals. For upstream contracts \\(Y_d\\) and downstream contracts \\(Z_d\\), the DSO's utility function is given by, \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\in Z_d}p_\\omega - \\sum_{(\\phi,t_\\phi,p_\\phi)\\in Y_d}p_\\phi,\\) if \\(\\underline{P}^D_t \\le P^D_t-|Y_{dt}|+|Z_{dt}| \\le \\bar{P}^D_t, \\forall t\\in\\mathcal{T},\\) \\(U_d(Y_d\\cup Z_d)=-\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{5}\\)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e03","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"contracts associated with each time interval t ∈T , given by, ene ing Yit = {(φ, tφ, pφ) ∈Xi|b(φ) = ","equation_id":"(2)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"These can be further divided into upstream and downstream contracts associated with each time interval t \\in T, given by, Y_{it} = \\{(\\varphi, t_{\\varphi}, p_{\\varphi}) \\in X_i \\mid b(\\varphi)=i, t_{\\varphi}=t\\}, Z_{it} = \\{(\\omega, t_{\\omega}, p_{\\omega}) \\in X_i \\mid s(\\omega)=i, t_{\\omega}=t\\}. \\tag{2}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Aggregator Preferences","anchor_text":"C. Aggregator Preferences Let S ⊆A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators","equation_id":"(6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Let S \\subseteq A be the set of aggregators in the trading network. The aggregators act as intermediaries, buying and selling flexibility contracts from the DSO and prosumers. It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced. It is assumed that aggregator j \\in S incurs a transaction cost for each accepted upstream contract, c_{1j} \\ge 0. In the flexibility market, for upstream contracts Y_j and downstream contracts Z_j, aggregator j's utility function is given by, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = \\sum_{(\\omega,t_{\\omega},p_{\\omega})\\in Z_j} p_{\\omega} - \\sum_{(\\varphi,t_{\\varphi},p_{\\varphi})\\in Y_j} p_{\\varphi} - c_{1j}|Y_j|, \\text{if } |Y_{jt}|=|Z_{jt}|, \\forall t\\in T, U_j(Y_j \\cup Z_j) = -\\infty, \\text{otherwise}. \\tag{6}"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":null,"empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-bidding","v141-a-M01-clearing","v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"clearing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","quote":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e19","quote":"The fact that the agent preferences are fully substitutable has two further consequences. First, the trading network can reach agreement on a stable outcome using the deferred-acceptance agent-to-agent price-negotiation mechanism from [54]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","quote":"Each agent i \\in A has a utility function U_i which describes its preferences over sets of contracts. The subset of contracts selected by the agent are given by its choice correspondence, C_i(X) = \\operatorname*{argmax}_{X'_i \\subseteq X_i} U_i(X'_i). \\tag{3}"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e13","quote":"For a trading network with contracts X, an outcome is a feasible set of contracts X' \\subseteq X, where X' is feasible if each trade \\omega \\in \\tau(X') is associated with at most one contract, and therefore has a unique price."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","quote":"Even when acting autonomously and making individually rational decisions, the DSO, aggregators and prosumers should reach agreement on a stable outcome—a set of individually beneficial transactions no group wishes to mutually deviate from."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","quote":"In other words, an outcome is stable as long as no agent would prefer to drop a contract they are associated with, and no group of agents would prefer to mutually deviate to a different set of contracts."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","quote":"Market outcomes should satisfy Pareto efficiency, meaning that it should not be possible to make one participant better off without making another worse off."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","quote":"The prosumers and DSO have maximum power limits. For each time interval when their maximum power limit is violated, they should select upstream contracts starting with the lowest priced contracts, until the violation is cleared. Similarly, high priced downstream contracts should be selected for each time interval until their minimum power limits are not violated. The prosumers also need to ensure their energy storage constraints are not violated."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e25","quote":"Note that if the prosumer resources have sufficient flexibility to satisfy the DSO's demand constraints, then the constraints will be satisfied at the end of the price-negotiation."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","quote":"The trading network has a single DSO, d \\in A, which must manage minimum and maximum limits on the total distribution network demand during each time interval"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E27","quote":"It is assumed that the aggregators do not consume, store or generate energy. Therefore, their total upstream and downstream contracts for each time interval must be balanced."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","quote":"The aggregator preferences can be extended to handle situations where the aggregators have their own flexible energy resources (e.g. community-scale energy storage systems [50]). In this case, the upstream-downstream contract balance constraint \\(|Y_{jt}| = |Z_{jt}|, \\forall t \\in \\mathcal{T}\\) would be replaced by the power and energy constraints from the prosumer preferences (4)."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","quote":"Given the single improvement property, the agents can use the following process to select utility-maximizing contract subsets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e27","quote":"Once the agents have selected the minimum number of contracts necessary to meet their constraints, they should select additional contracts which increase their utility. Iteratively, the agents need to assess the marginal utility provided by each of the lowest priced upstream contracts still available at each time interval, the highest priced downstream contracts still available at each interval and upstream-downstream pairs of these contracts."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e28","quote":"The option or options providing the greatest increase in utility should be selected. When none of these options provides further utility, a utility maximizing subset of contracts has been selected."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"783cae3a3e3b476fb274be965d9bbbf8f659e682c4001e078c245d1549e42bff","applicability":"formalized","reason":"C.1 supplied no typed x/theta and marked every phi entry procedural. C.2 adds only source-defined contract-set, demand and limit objects and formalizes the choice, feasibility, stability, prosumer power/energy, DSO demand and aggregator balance constraints directly from the supplied equations (2)-(6) and Definition 1. C001, C002, C006, C010 and C011 remain procedural or qualitative because the supplied text states process or property requirements without a closed-form expression. B evidence remains unreviewed.","bindings":[{"object_id":"x-Xprime","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The selected contract set X' is the local clearing outcome; no separate graph edge is needed for an internal outcome set."},{"object_id":"x-Xprime-i","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Agent i's selected subset X'_i is the internal contract-selection decision within local clearing."},{"object_id":"x-Yit","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Per-interval upstream contract sets are inputs to prosumer feasibility constraints inside local clearing."},{"object_id":"x-Zit","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Per-interval downstream contract sets are inputs to prosumer feasibility constraints inside local clearing."},{"object_id":"x-Ydt","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"DSO per-interval upstream contracts enter the local demand-feasibility condition."},{"object_id":"x-Zdt","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"DSO per-interval downstream contracts enter the local demand-feasibility condition."},{"object_id":"x-Yjt","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Aggregator per-interval upstream contracts enter the local contract-balance condition."},{"object_id":"x-Zjt","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Aggregator per-interval downstream contracts enter the local contract-balance condition."},{"object_id":"theta-Xi","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The available contract set X_i is an input to the choice correspondence used during local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-Ui","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The utility function is an input to the choice correspondence and stability test used in local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-Pf-it","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The pre-market planned flexible demand P^f_it is a fixed input to prosumer feasibility in local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-Ei0","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Initial stored energy E_i0 is a fixed input to the prosumer energy-storage constraint in local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-Pf-lower","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Lower power limits are fixed inputs to prosumer contract-selection feasibility."},{"object_id":"theta-Pf-upper","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Upper power limits are fixed inputs to prosumer contract-selection feasibility."},{"object_id":"theta-E-lower","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Lower energy-storage limits are fixed inputs to prosumer contract-selection feasibility."},{"object_id":"theta-E-upper","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Upper energy-storage limits are fixed inputs to prosumer contract-selection feasibility."},{"object_id":"theta-PD-t","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Predicted pre-flexibility retail demand is a fixed input to the DSO demand-feasibility condition in local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-PD-lower","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The minimum distribution-network demand limit is a fixed input to the DSO feasibility condition in local clearing."},{"object_id":"theta-PD-upper","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The maximum distribution-network demand limit is a fixed input to the DSO feasibility condition in local clearing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C003","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Equation (3) defines the utility-maximizing contract-subset selection internal to local clearing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C004","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The at-most-one-contract-per-trade condition is an internal feasibility test on the local clearing outcome."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C005","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Definition 1 supplies the stability test applied to the local clearing outcome; no separate graph edge represents this internal validation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C007","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Prosumer power and energy limits constrain contract selection inside local clearing."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C008","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"DSO demand bounds constrain the final local clearing allocation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R002-C009","node_ids":["clearing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Aggregator per-interval upstream-downstream balance constrains local contract selection."}],"revision_requests":["C001/C002 price-negotiation termination and deferred-acceptance steps would require explicit iteration-indexed buyer/seller price objects and a negotiation-state representation; the frozen rule graph has no such objects, so C.2 leaves them procedural.","C006 Pareto efficiency cannot be encoded with clearing.local alone; a welfare or utility-comparison representation is needed if the source's qualitative efficiency requirement is to be formalized.","C010 extension and C011 local-search selection algorithm need explicit source-defined adjustment/neighborhood operators and replacement constraints before they can be transcribed as formulas.","B re-extraction should resolve OCR/notation ambiguity in equation (4) energy-limit indices (E_{it} versus \\underline{E}_{i\\tau}) and confirm whether P^{D}_{t} uses per-contract units; the supplied B evidence is unreviewed."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
